        {
            "id": "26954617",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "11:59, 16 August 2019",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(5,719 bytes)",
            "change": 402,
            "summary": "(Added sources for the wikihaus content team.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Unwind several feet of the light string and feed a little bit of it into one end of the pipe.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c6y5gyldRDE</ref> [[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Start up the vacuum cleaner and attach it to the other end of the pipe. Try to seal the connection the best you can to make the most efficient use of the vacuum. The vacuum should pull the string through the pipe.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c6y5gyldRDE</ref> .[[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Once the heavier string / rope has been pulled through the pipe, attach your final wire or cable, and pull this through manually. If needing to pull more than one to three smaller wires, connect wires in such a way that the point of connection to the heavy string is not a single \"clump of wires\" folded over.  This will make for a difficult \"pull\" through the conduit.  Instead, connect only one wire directly to the string, create one or more loops in this wire 6 - 8 or more inches down the wire from the connection point.  Slip any additional wire(s) several inches into the loop, fold over and wrap around the first wire.  Repeat this as often as required and wrap electrical tape around the string / rope just above the connection point all the way down just beyond the last wrap of the last wire hooked through the loops.  The result should look like the end of a pencil and will pass much easier through the conduit.<ref>https://diy.stackexchange.com/questions/163799/tv-cables-getting-stuck-in-conduit-after-mounting-tv-on-fireplace</ref> [[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a metal or fiberglass fish tape or snake.  If the conduit run is long, has several bends, has more than 25% of its area filled with wires, etc., the strength and flexibility offered by a fish tape or snake will make pushing into the conduit much easier.  Often times it can be used to pull new wires directly into the pipe - saving time of multiple pulls of increasing strength string / rope.  Fiberglass does not conduct electricity and is highly recommended for use instead of metal fish tapes and snakes.<ref>https://www.electricalknowledge.com/electricians-tools/what-is-fish-tape/</ref> [[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "*A spinning reel is a great source of easy feed \"string\".<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3Wk4TsBJEgk</ref>",
                    "",
                    "==References==",
                    "{{reflist}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Unwind several feet of the light string and feed a little bit of it into one end of the pipe.[[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Start up the vacuum cleaner and attach it to the other end of the pipe. Try to seal the connection the best you can to make the most efficient use of the vacuum. The vacuum should pull the string through the pipe..[[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Once the heavier string / rope has been pulled through the pipe, attach your final wire or cable, and pull this through manually. If needing to pull more than one to three smaller wires, connect wires in such a way that the point of connection to the heavy string is not a single \"clump of wires\" folded over.  This will make for a difficult \"pull\" through the conduit.  Instead, connect only one wire directly to the string, create one or more loops in this wire 6 - 8 or more inches down the wire from the connection point.  Slip any additional wire(s) several inches into the loop, fold over and wrap around the first wire.  Repeat this as often as required and wrap electrical tape around the string / rope just above the connection point all the way down just beyond the last wrap of the last wire hooked through the loops.  The result should look like the end of a pencil and will pass much easier through the conduit.[[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a metal or fiberglass fish tape or snake.  If the conduit run is long, has several bends, has more than 25% of its area filled with wires, etc., the strength and flexibility offered by a fish tape or snake will make pushing into the conduit much easier.  Often times it can be used to pull new wires directly into the pipe - saving time of multiple pulls of increasing strength string / rope.  Fiberglass does not conduct electricity and is highly recommended for use instead of metal fish tapes and snakes.[[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "*A spinning reel is a great source of easy feed \"string\"."
                ]
            }
        },
