                "removed": [
                    "#  [[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 1.jpg|right|300px]]Unwind several feet of the light string and feed a little bit of it into one end of the pipe.",
                    "#  [[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 2.jpg|right|300px]]Start up the vacuum cleaner and attach it to the other end of the pipe. Try to seal the connection the best you can to make the most efficient use of the vacuum. The vacuum should pull the string through the pipe.",
                    "#  [[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 3.jpg|right|300px]]Once you have pulled the light string through the pipe, attach a heavier string / rope to the end, and pull this through manually.",
                    "#  [[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 4.jpg|right|300px]]Once the heavier string / rope has been pulled through the pipe, attach your final wire or cable, and pull this through manually. If needing to pull more than one to three smaller wires, connect wires in such a way that the point of connection to the heavy string is not a single \"clump of wires\" folded over.  This will make for a difficult \"pull\" through the conduit.  Instead, connect only one wire directly to the string, create one or more loops in this wire 6 - 8 or more inches down the wire from the connection point.  Slip any additional wire(s) several inches into the loop, fold over and wrap around the first wire.  Repeat this as often as required and wrap electrical tape around the string / rope just above the connection point all the way down just beyond the last wrap of the last wire hooked through the loops.  The result should look like the end of a pencil and will pass much easier through the conduit.",
                    "#  [[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 5.jpg|right|300px]]Use a metal or fiberglass fish tape or snake.  If the conduit run is long, has several bends, has more than 25% of its area filled with wires, etc., the strength and flexibility offered by a fish tape or snake will make pushing into the conduit much easier.  Often times it can be used to pull new wires directly into the pipe - saving time of multiple pulls of increasing strength string / rope.  Fiberglass does not conduct electricity and is highly recommended for use instead of metal fish tapes and snakes."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "9308591",
            "user": "Wikiphoto",
            "timestamp": "16:16, 1 March 2013",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(5,594 bytes)",
            "change": 282,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "[[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Intro.jpg|right|300px]]Suppose you have an existing pipe or conduit for electrical wires, speaker wires, phone or internet cables.  Trying to push something through the pipe will undoubtedly just leave you frustrated.  Pulling is the only answer.  But, how do you pull another wire through that pipe?",
                    "#  [[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 1.jpg|right|300px]]Unwind several feet of the light string and feed a little bit of it into one end of the pipe.",
                    "#  [[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 2.jpg|right|300px]]Start up the vacuum cleaner and attach it to the other end of the pipe. Try to seal the connection the best you can to make the most efficient use of the vacuum. The vacuum should pull the string through the pipe.",
                    "#  [[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 3.jpg|right|300px]]Once you have pulled the light string through the pipe, attach a heavier string / rope to the end, and pull this through manually.",
                    "#  [[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 4.jpg|right|300px]]Once the heavier string / rope has been pulled through the pipe, attach your final wire or cable, and pull this through manually. If needing to pull more than one to three smaller wires, connect wires in such a way that the point of connection to the heavy string is not a single \"clump of wires\" folded over.  This will make for a difficult \"pull\" through the conduit.  Instead, connect only one wire directly to the string, create one or more loops in this wire 6 - 8 or more inches down the wire from the connection point.  Slip any additional wire(s) several inches into the loop, fold over and wrap around the first wire.  Repeat this as often as required and wrap electrical tape around the string / rope just above the connection point all the way down just beyond the last wrap of the last wire hooked through the loops.  The result should look like the end of a pencil and will pass much easier through the conduit.",
                    "#  [[Image:Fish Wires Through a Conduit or Pipe Step 5.jpg|right|300px]]Use a metal or fiberglass fish tape or snake.  If the conduit run is long, has several bends, has more than 25% of its area filled with wires, etc., the strength and flexibility offered by a fish tape or snake will make pushing into the conduit much easier.  Often times it can be used to pull new wires directly into the pipe - saving time of multiple pulls of increasing strength string / rope.  Fiberglass does not conduct electricity and is highly recommended for use instead of metal fish tapes and snakes.",
                    "*If there is already another wire or cable inside the conduit, then it is often simpler to disconnect it and use it as the \"pull string\".  Connect the new wires PLUS two wires.  The first extra wire is to replace the wire being used as a pull string and the second is to be left in the conduit as a pull string for possible later use.  You can inspect the wire that was removed for suitability for later reuse or scrap."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "[[Image:Electrical.jpg|thumb|right|251px]]Suppose you have an existing pipe or conduit for electrical wires, speaker wires, phone or internet cables.  Trying to push something through the pipe will undoubtedly just leave you frustrated.  Pulling is the only answer.  But, how do you pull another wire through that pipe?",
                    "#  Unwind several feet of the light string and feed a little bit of it into one end of the pipe.",
                    "#  Start up the vacuum cleaner and attach it to the other end of the pipe. Try to seal the connection the best you can to make the most efficient use of the vacuum. The vacuum should pull the string through the pipe.",
                    "#  Once you have pulled the light string through the pipe, attach a heavier string / rope to the end, and pull this through manually.",
                    "#  Once the heavier string / rope has been pulled through the pipe, attach your final wire or cable, and pull this through manually. If needing to pull more than one to three smaller wires, connect wires in such a way that the point of connection to the heavy string is not a single \"clump of wires\" folded over.  This will make for a difficult \"pull\" through the conduit.  Instead, connect only one wire directly to the string, create one or more loops in this wire 6 - 8 or more inches down the wire from the connection point.  Slip any additional wire(s) several inches into the loop, fold over and wrap around the first wire.  Repeat this as often as required and wrap electrical tape around the string / rope just above the connection point all the way down just beyond the last wrap of the last wire hooked through the loops.  The result should look like the end of a pencil and will pass much easier through the conduit.",
                    "#  Use a metal or fiberglass fish tape or snake.  If the conduit run is long, has several bends, has more than 25% of its area filled with wires, etc., the strength and flexibility offered by a fish tape or snake will make pushing into the conduit much easier.  Often times it can be used to pull new wires directly into the pipe - saving time of multiple pulls of increasing strength string / rope.  Fiberglass does not conduct electricity and is highly recommended for use instead of metal fish tapes and snakes.",
                    "*If there is already another wire or cable inside the conduit, then it is often simpler to disconnect it and use it as the \"pull string\".  Connect the new wires PLUS two wires.  The first extra wire is to replace the wire being used as a pull string and the second is to be left in the conduit as a pull string for possible later use.  You can inspect the wire that was removed for suitability for later reuse or scrap.[[Image:WireLube.JPG|thumb|right|192px|Two different types of lubricant  made by Ideal.]]"
                ]
            }
        },
